      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the values of \\( y \\) for which the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined, we need to find the values of \\( y \\) that make the denominator equal to zero. The expression will be undefined when the denominator is zero because division by zero is not defined.\n\nThe denominator of the expression is \\( y^2 - 5y + 4 \\). We need to solve the equation:\n\\[ y^2 - 5y + 4 = 0. \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, and we can solve it by factoring. We look for two numbers that multiply to \\( 4 \\) (the constant term) and add up to \\( -5 \\) (the coefficient of the linear term). These two numbers are \\( -1 \\) and \\( -4 \\) because:\n\\[ -1 \\times -4 = 4 \\]\n\\[ -1 + (-4) = -5. \\]\n\nSo, we can factor the quadratic equation as:\n\\[ y^2 - 5y + 4 = (y - 1)(y - 4). \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\\[ y - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y - 4 = 0, \\]\n\\[ y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 4. \\]\n\nTherefore, the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined for \\( y = 1 \\) and \\( y = 4 \\). To find the sum of all such values of \\( y \\), we add these solutions together:\n\\[ 1 + 4 = 5. \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all values of \\( y \\) for which the expression is undefined is:\n\\[ \\boxed{5}. \\]",
