    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine when the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined, we need to find the values of \\(y\\) that cause the denominator to be zero because division by zero is undefined.\n\nLet's start by setting up the denominator:\n\\[\ny^2 - 5y + 4\n\\]\n\nWe aim to identify the roots of this quadratic equation:\n\\[\ny^2 - 5y + 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo solve this quadratic equation, we can factorize it. We need to find two numbers that multiply to the constant term (4) and add up to the coefficient of the linear term (-5). These numbers are -1 and -4 because:\n\\[\n-1 \\times -4 = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -1 + (-4) = -5\n\\]\n\nThus, we can factor the quadratic as:\n\\[\ny^2 - 5y + 4 = (y - 1)(y - 4) = 0\n\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the roots:\n\\[\ny - 1 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 1\n\\]\n\\[\ny - 4 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4\n\\]\n\nThese are the values of \\(y\\) that make the denominator zero, and hence, the expression is undefined.\n\nTo find the sum of these values, we add them:\n\\[\n1 + 4 = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all values of \\(y\\) for which the expression \\(\\frac{y+6}{y^2-5y+4}\\) is undefined is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
